 and so and then  Lee mentally was the art  gallery curator at the community center  on the top floor gallery and we already  knew him from the children's theater and  so we all came together and he said why  don't you rent we'll rent you a room on  the second floor of the gay community  center you can do your dying there  here's the room it had a stove it had  running water but no hot water but we  used to just heat it up in canning pots  and then I'd mix in giant trash cans in  that room with that had a large table  too and make a big mess and then I had  to run off to the wash mat laundromat to  wash everything they were making  arrangements to put the parade on you  know I think it was probably leave asked  me do I want to be part of the  decorations committee because I'm right  there every day so yeah of course  Gilbert came along he he came along  during this period but there was another  a third person that's got no credit and  he passed away from AIDS his name's  James McNamara and he's the guy that  taught Gilbert how to so he went to fi t  he knew how to so beautifully in pattern  he made his own clothing line and he was  a mentor to me we had a little meeting  of three people to decide what the  decorations would be so I had some  sketches or something and this idea of  making rainbow flags because at the time  I was very Argyle rainbow and I did a  lot of rainbows and that was really my  thing  so everybody loved it you know and I  also knew how to make flags I had  already made flags for a sailboat  company they said oh we'll give you the  reflecting pool had rows of flag poles  along it at the city hall and then  they'll give you these two big flag  poles at UN Plaza so we need two big  ones and then we need these smaller ones  and they were gonna be each individual  artist like you get a flag you get a  flag you get a flag here's your white  flag we've stitched them up and now it's  up to you to color this however you want  is your project we need them back by  this time and then the two big flag  poles were like James and I were like  okay  you know let's figure out what to do Oh  the year before there was a pride parade  in San Francisco and some special you  know private people had made little  flags of their own but they weren't  rainbow flags they're like a purple  triangle and some other a friend of mine  has a photograph of the year before  there's just like a couple of little  things that people would hold in their  hands as they walked you know our  homemade banner downstairs on the second  floor is where the parade committee was  working like doing like this where the  stage where's the route where is the  permits where is the porta-potties all  the practical stuff does she need if  you're gonna put on a parade they were  busy with that you know it was the place  was jamming I think we had about six  weeks to get it together once we decided  on this and got all the materials all  the fabrics 1,000 yards of white cotton  muslin and the dyes salt so - the trash  cans that all the things and that we  brought our sewing machines we had 3  sewing machines three ironing boards  three irons and all the little stuff the  main three people were James Gilbert and  I but I found that once I started the  dyeing I was so busy dying I didn't have  time for sewing so someone just used my  sewing machine so it's not like cut and  dried  Oh meeting it - I didn't even have a  telephone you know you can find me here  my studio was on the second floor and we  started in there we were making such a  mess and there's no drains in the floor  there so Lee said why don't we do it on  the roof because there's a chip on the  top of the roof of the gay community  center there's this water main for the  firemen it just pours out cold water and  there's these giant drains in the roof  so we brought our trash cans up there  and then we that was off the third floor  you had to go up this staircase that was  really like a giant ladder so we heated  water up in giant canning pots on the  second floor carried him up to the third  floor then carried him up to the roof  dumped it in the trash cans along with  cold water mixed it and then the daya  salt and soda ash and  and mix to mix and then the fabric and  so that's where helpers not only were  they downstairs ironing fabric after it  been dyed and washed everything had to  be ironed and helping the seamstresses  tailors and then mostly it was Glen she  was working with me at the dye trashcans  I was the first person that was running  the stuff to the wash mat laundromat  with coiours and big giant garbage bags  to wash it then they're like oh you're  wasting time we need you upstairs so  here we'll get these people to do that  it was all figuring out how many yards  of this because the the two large ones  were forty feet by sixty feet on 80-foot  flag poles just very big they were large  and when they were finished it took  about three people to carry one flag the  two flags had eight colors yeah it was  pink red orange yellow green aqua blue  royal blue and violet and then mine had  stars in the corner for to emulate the  American flag and I reversed the colors  on the two flags this is on purpose it  so if you look carefully at the any  photos from that day  pink is at the bottom on one flag and  violet at the top and the other flag has  it reversed  we had to rig the flags get the person  with a key to the flag pulls the crank  to the flag poles and the trot her truck  and six of us or eight of us get them  over there and then clip them and take  the wire rigging that goes through the  end of the flag and it's clipped on both  ends and then you know put it up and  then crank crank crank and get it to go  up to the top and do the other one the  same thing bring it  and then crank crank crank and see if  they came out okay that was a I think  the day before so then we took it all  down we went back to the gay community  center obviously we were up early and we  got up the flags back in the truck and  all went over there and because we'd  have to get those up before the parade  started obviously and it's a big job and  so they went up and it looked great  I wanted a full spectrum rainbow I  wanted all the colors and for me it  meant everybody is included everybody  it's not like oh the color of skin no  everybody likes rainbows we're at the  gay community center and there's  everybody is there I mean you don't have  to be gay to be there but you know if  you are it's great and you know like  come on over and then we had to get  arranged we walked in the parade and so  in Gilbert and James and I and I guess  probably all of us and it was a great  day then within a year from 1978 gay  freedom day to the next year the flags  were stolen the two giant flags and that  was just like I was just such a shock  that I couldn't believe it that was just  our heartbreaker the gay community  center got torn down and made into a  parking lot  AIDS epidemic was happening people are  strung out on heavy drugs and a lot of  other bad stuff and it was just like  dark really dark it was a very sad time  we all left because there was no place  to be and I think it took it took years  before there was another gay community  center in San Francisco and now I  continue to dye fabrics for a living but  Gilbert he wanted to you know keep  putting the flags up and promoting and  so the next year he got together  polyester flags they made polyester  flags he's the person that promoted it  and popularized it and promoted it and  pounding the pavement and brought out  the flags for all sorts of events and  there was no Stars and Stripes when  she doesn't know how to do that so I'm  really glad it came to be a big huge  symbol and Gilbert's directly  responsible for doing that but I always  felt like he should give James and I do  credit you know whenever I see a rainbow  flag it makes me feel good you know I  was directly involved and 